  "final": {
    "evidence_sufficiency": "SUFFICIENT",
    "confidence": 88,
    "conclusion": "The available evidence supports local, dimension-specific response sensitivity rather than meaningful semantic instability under nominally identical questioning. Exact repeats produced no semantic-class variation across 16 anchors, although broader response-state features could vary. Small prompt paraphrases and added prior-answer context reliably increased full-state distance while preserving semantic class; the observed changes chiefly involved claim state, action, evidence, uncertainty, assertion, referent, and abstention/request behavior. Thus, explanations based on inherent semantic-class randomness under exact repetition should be rejected for these data, while explanations based on sensitivity to prompt wording and conversational history should be retained. Conclusions about lower-level dimensions—especially referent, abstention, claim state, and exact evidence sets—should remain qualified because their measurement reliability was only moderate, weak, or poor, whereas semantic-class and action findings are comparatively well supported."
